            "body": "## Summary\n\nReplacing the withdrawal regex with `passTailAllowed` is the right shape: extra prose after `PASS` (caveats, notes) no longer parses as a verdict, which closes holes the negation list could never enumerate. The SimiBot `mc-verdict` marker path is wired consistently with `noteUnparsed` when the first line does not parse.\n\nThis head still does **not** implement the pinned pointer rule for what may follow `PASS` on the first line.\n\n## Findings\n\n### P1 — `SHA_WORD` still accepts 7–11 character “pointers” (**measured**)\n\n`SHA_WORD` is `/^[0-9a-f]{7,40}$/i` (lines 227–228 at head). The same module’s `shaClaimedByComment` documentation already requires **12–39** for abbreviated claims (and explains why seven hex digits are grindable). Prefixes shorter than 12 must not count as a pointer on the PASS line; today `PASS effaced` (7) and `PASS 2dee2723` (8) would still pass `passTailAllowed` when they appear as the bare first tail word.\n\n### P1 — Hex-looking English words still parse as PASS (**inferred** from the same rule)\n\nBecause any 7–40 hex run is accepted as a bare tail word, author-realistic lines such as `PASS deadbeef` or `PASS effaced` still yield a verdict token. Those are not head pointers under the pinned rule even when they match `[0-9a-f]`.\n\n**Suggested alignment:** treat a bare tail token as a pointer only at length 40; for 12–39 require the existing `at` / `at head` / `head` / `on` introducers (matching how claims work elsewhere in this file). Reject 7–11 character bare tokens entirely.\n\n### P1 — Empty tail still counts as PASS (**inferred** from `passTailAllowed`)\n\nWhen the tail is empty, `words` is `[]`, `i` stays 0, and `return i === words.length` is true. A first line that is only `PASS` (or `PASS` plus punctuation) therefore still parses, but the pinned rule requires a full 40-character SHA or a ≥12 hex prefix that resolves to the head—silence is not a pointer.\n\n### P2 — Signature-only tail without a pointer (**inferred**)\n\n`PASS -simibot` / `PASS grokbot` with no SHA satisfies `passTailAllowed` (signature consumes the only word). The pinned rule ties PASS to a head pointer first; signature alone should not salvage the line.\n\n### P2 — Resolution to head is not enforced on the PASS-line pointer (**measured**)\n\n`passTailAllowed` is purely syntactic. Wrong-but-plausible 12+ prefixes that never resolve via `resolveSha` in `collectPinnedVerdicts` should not count as PASS at parse time if the product rule is “resolves to the head.” At minimum, document whether resolution is intentionally deferred; otherwise thread `resolveSha` (or equivalent) into the check for non-40 prefixes.\n\n### Not a regression — intentional tightening (**measured** vs base)\n\nBase deliberately kept some caveat tails (e.g. “PASS, but docs lag”) as PASS via absent `but`/`not` patterns. The new allowlist rejects those tails. That matches the PR comment and is consistent with fail-closed parsing.\n\n## What already works (**inferred**)\n\n- `PASS, but …` / extra words after a valid pointer+signature pattern: rejected (`i !== words.length`).\n- `PASS deadbeef/effaced` as one slash-glued token: `SHA_WORD` fails → no verdict.\n- `PASS at head <40>` / `PASS head <sha>` with valid SHA shape: accepted.\n\n## Ask\n\nTighten `SHA_WORD` / bare-tail handling to 40-only bare or 12–39 with introducers; require a non-empty valid pointer before optional signature; add table-driven tests for the pinned examples (`deadbeef`, `effaced`, short prefixes, bare `PASS`, valid 12+ and full SHAs).",
