e `%s` interpolation — there is no chunking. `|removed| ≤ |self._snapshot.netuids|` because `removed` is a subset of the prior snapshot’s netuids.\n\n**inferred:** There is no new cap in this diff; membership size is not limited to `MAX_NETUIDS` (200) — that constant bounds probe scan width, while TMC pagination can admit more members. In the worst case, if every prior member is eligible and dropped in one allowed refresh, one log line could list **all former members** (on the order of the live subnet count, potentially well over 200 integers in one list repr).\n\n## Review\n\n**measured:** Read `refresh_once`, `_shrink_allowed`, `_eligible_for_removal`, and `STALE_GRACE_SECONDS` (172800) at head `3d2338b70231def2b55252c1d130c3bec27b1ed8`.\n\n**inferred:** No path where this log aborts a cycle, poisons a later row, prevents expiry, or performs float overflow; it is observability only after the existing grace and shrink gates.\n\n| Skill | Found | Changed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| adversarial-review | Traced `refresh_once` → `_builder.build` → `_shrink_allowed` / `_hold_prior`; log only on allowed shrink with non-empty set difference | — |\n| owner-rules | Operational info log; no secret values in format; aligns with existing removed-netuid logging | — |\n| settled-check | Same `removed` list pattern already used on unsafe-shrink warning; not a new policy | — |",
