            "body": "MODIFY\n\nHead `8696641c233071dab4f6d2976610b82e4d4e76a3`. One file: `review-gate-bot/bot/lib/tag.ts` (−16 lines).\n\n## What changed (measured)\n\n1. **`postSlotOnce`**: removed the pre-`createComment` `isStale(pulls.get)` check. Tags (`@cursor`/`@ditto`, `@replit`) now go straight from the kv `ranOnce` claim to `postComment`.\n2. **`postVerdictComment`**: removed guard 3 — the in-`run` `alreadyPosted` re-read and the `lostToRecheck` / `already-posted` early return. Verdict posts still call `isStale` immediately before `createComment`.\n\nEntry point unchanged: channel hooks and tools still call `tagCursorAndDitto`, `tagReplit`, and `postVerdictComment` in `tag.ts`; coordination remains `ranOnce` in `lock.ts` (measured: hosted kv has no CAS; claim is get/put/read-back best effort).\n\n## Findings\n\n### P1 — Stale tags after push or draft flip (inferred)\n\n`isStale`’s own comment (still at head) says it must be asked **as the last thing before a write**, because a `@cursor review` after a push would ask reviewers to act on an old head while the gate decision was for that old head (measured text at lines 57–62). Base had that check inside `postSlotOnce`; head does not.\n\n`tagCursorAndDitto` only receives a caller-supplied `GateState` and `headSha` — no `currentHeadSha` re-read in the post path (measured). So the window the doc describes (several gate round trips, then write) is exactly where the removed `pulls.get` sat.\n\nIf the PR moves to a new SHA or turns draft after the gate read but before the tag comment lands, head can still post a marker for the **old** SHA. That does not satisfy `alreadyPosted` for the **new** head (marker is SHA-scoped, measured in `gate.ts` / `alreadyPosted`), so the gate on the new head can remain stuck in `await_cursor_ditto` or `tag_replit` while reviewers get pinged for the wrong commit. This is the push-in-flight scenario called out in the PR context; dropping the read trades it away without a replacement guard on the tag path.\n\n`postSlotOnce` still documents `superseded` and `tagOutcome` still maps it to `gate-not-ready`/`blocked`, but `superseded` is never set on the tag path at head (measured) — dead logic and misleading comments.\n\n### P2 — Wider cross-process duplicate MC verdict window (inferred)\n\n`lock.ts` documents that without CAS two processes can both win a claim (measured). Guard 3 existed to shrink the gap between “claimed” and `createComment` when the other process finished posting in between. Removing it leaves: `alreadyDone` at start of `ranOnce`, kv claim, `isStale`, post — no final comment-list re-read.\n\nThe file still states guard 3 is what makes replays safe and that duplicate comments at one head leave the **latest** verdict winning (measured lines 213–234). With two different bodies in a race, `verdictAt` / gate `next` can follow the wrong line (inferred from `gate.ts` “latest one wins”). That window is widened by this diff, not narrowed.\n\n### P3 — Documentation still describes removed behavior (measured)\n\nAt head, module comment still says `postVerdictComment` re-reads immediately before posting (lines 36–39). `postVerdictComment` block still lists **three** guards including the comment re-read (guard 3). Implementation no longer has guard 3. `isStale` is still described as “last thing before **a** write” but tags no longer call it.\n\nIf the latency trade is intentional, the comments must be rewritten so operators and future diffs do not assume guards that are not in code (owner expectation: do not pretend the CAS/window is closed when it is not).\n\n## What would unblock PASS\n\n- Restore `isStale` in `postSlotOnce` **or** an equivalent last-moment head/draft check on the tag write path, and wire `superseded` again **or** delete the dead `superseded`/`tagOutcome` branch and fix docs.\n- Either restore the pre-post `alreadyPosted` re-read for verdicts **or** update all guard documentation (including module comment and, in a follow-up, `lock.ts`’s reference to `postVerdictComment` re-reading) to describe the actual two-guard behavior and the accepted duplicate-verdict risk.\n\n## Severity note\n\nVerdict posts retain `isStale`; the worst push-in-flight case for **MC verdict** posting is partially mitigated. Tag posting is not.",
