      "finalText": "**MODIFY** posted for head `8696641c233071dab4f6d2976610b82e4d4e76a3`.\n\nThe PR removes two pre-write GitHub reads in `tag.ts`: `isStale` before tag comments in `postSlotOnce`, and the final `alreadyPosted` re-read (guard 3) before MC verdict posts. Saving latency is clear; in a multi-process, no-CAS, push-while-posting setup, that trades away protections the file still documents.\n\n**Stale tags (P1, inferred):** Without `isStale` on the tag path, a push or draft flip between the gate read and `createComment` can still emit `@cursor` / `@replit` markers for an old SHA. That does not satisfy the new head’s gate, but it pings reviewers for the wrong commit—the scenario `isStale`’s comment describes. `superseded` / `tagOutcome` still talk about that case but are never set on tags at head.\n\n**Verdict races (P2, inferred):** Dropping guard 3 widens the window where two processes can both post MC verdicts; the gate keeps the latest marker, so conflicting bodies can steer `next` wrong. Comments still describe three guards and guard 3 as replay protection.\n\n**Docs (P3, measured):** Module and `postVerdictComment` docs still claim a pre-post comment re-read and that `isStale` is the last check before “a write,” while tags no longer call it and guard 3 is gone.\n\n**Partial mitigation (measured):** `postVerdictComment` still runs `isStale` before posting; only the tag path and the verdict duplicate guard were removed.\n\nTo reach **PASS**: restore those checks or replace them with an equivalent last-moment head/draft guard on tags, and either restore the verdict re-read or rewrite the guard docs to match the accepted risk. Full write-up is on the PR via `post_verdict`.",
