            "body": "## Summary\n\nThis PR replaces the withdrawal-regex approach with an explicit `passTailAllowed` allowlist on the PASS tail, which is the right direction for fail-closed parsing (**inferred** from file comments and diff). At head `2dee2723c015ef610ce36da8c0afdb37aed61c0a`, the tail checker still accepts SHA-shaped tokens that the pinned rule and the same module’s pinning rules treat as non-pointers.\n\n## Findings\n\n### P1 — `SHA_WORD` still allows 7–39 character “bare” tails (MODIFY)\n\n**Measured:** `SHA_WORD = /^[0-9a-f]{7,40}$/i` and `passTailAllowed` treats a lone word matching that regex as a valid PASS tail (bare SHA path, or after `at` / `head` / `on`).\n\n**Measured:** `shaClaimedByComment` documentation in the same file states abbreviated claims use **12–39** hex characters and explicitly rejects seven-character prefixes as too collision-prone.\n\n**Inferred:** A first line like `PASS 2dee272` (7 hex chars) would pass `passTailAllowed` today but is not a valid head pointer under the pinned rule (prefix must be ≥12 and resolve to head) or under the file’s own 12-character claim policy.\n\n### P1 — Hex-looking English words count as pointers without resolution (MODIFY)\n\n**Measured:** `passTailAllowed` does not call `resolveSha` or otherwise require that a tail token names the PR head; format-only `SHA_WORD` is enough.\n\n**Inferred:** Lines such as `PASS deadbeef`, `PASS effaced`, or `PASS at deadbeef` parse as PASS because those tokens match `[0-9a-f]{7,40}` even when they are dictionary words, not commit SHAs.\n\n**Inferred:** The pinned rule for this review requires that after PASS, only a full 40-character head SHA or a ≥12 hex prefix that **resolves to the head** counts; otherwise the PASS must not count (parser should treat the line as unparsed / no PASS).\n\n### P2 — Joined fake pointer form (likely OK)\n\n**Inferred:** A tail token `deadbeef/effaced` (single word) fails `SHA_WORD` because of `/`, so `passTailAllowed` rejects it