            "verdict": "MODIFY",
            "body": "## Summary\n\nThe diff drops two GitHub round-trips per tag/verdict write by removing `isStale` from `postSlotOnce` and the immediate `alreadyPosted` re-read (guard 3) from `postVerdictComment`. That matches the PR title, but it leaves **tag** posts exposed to the push-during-post race the description calls out, while **verdict** posts still call `isStale` inside `run`. Comments at head still describe three guards and a pre-write re-read that the code no longer performs.\n\n## Findings\n\n### P2 — Stale `@cursor` / `@replit` tags after synchronize (**inferred**)\n\n**measured:** At head, `postSlotOnce` sets `superseded = false` and never assigns true; `run` goes straight to `postComment` with `marker(slot, headSha)` (lines ~107–124 in `review-gate-bot/bot/lib/tag.ts`).\n\n**measured:** Base had `if (await isStale(...)) { superseded = true; return; }` before `postComment` in the same function (removed in this PR).\n\n**inferred:** If the channel read gate at `headSha` A and a push lands on B (or the PR turns draft) before `createComment`, a tag still goes out pinned to A. The slot comment on `postSlotOnce` still says that case must not post, but nothing enforces it. `tagOutcome`’s `superseded` branch is dead code.\n\n**inferred:** Gate logic pins tags/verdicts to exact SHAs (`verdictAt`, `replitTaggedForSha === headSha` in `gate.ts`), so a stale marker may not satisfy the *new* head’s gate, but it still triggers reviewer pings for a head that is no longer current — the failure mode the removed guard documented.\n\n### P2 — Documentation contradicts behavior (**measured**)\n\n**measured:** Module header still says `` `postVerdictComment` re-reads immediately before posting `` (`tag.ts` ~34–37).\n\n**measured:** `postVerdictComment` block still lists **three** guards including guard 3 “re-read of the PR's comments as the last thing before the post” (~224–244); guard 3 and `lostToRecheck` handling were deleted.\n\n**measured:** `lock.ts` ~27–28 still says callers re-read immediately before posting and names `postVerdictComment`; this PR does not touch `lock.ts`.\n\n**measured:** `postSlotOnce` doc still defines `superseded` as head/draft drift before write; implementation no longer checks.\n\n### P3 — Wider cross-process duplicate MC verdict window (**inferred**)\n\n**measured:** `ranOnce` + non-CAS `claim` is documented best-effort across processes (`lock.ts`). Guard 3 narrowed the window between claim and `createComment` to one comment-list read adjacent to the write; removing it is intentional cost/latency trade.\n\n**inferred:** Two processes can still both post MC verdicts for the same head; the gate keeps the latest marker at that SHA. Same verdict twice is noisy; two different tokens let the later comment win — already acknowledged in the stale doc block. Acceptable only if comments are updated to say guard 3 is gone.\n\n## What would unblock PASS\n\n1. Either restore `isStale` (or equivalent single PR-head read) immediately before tag `createComment`, **or** update `postSlotOnce` / `tagOutcome` docs and callers to state that tag posts may target a superseded head when synchronize wins the race.\n2. Align all comments (`tag.ts` module header, `postVerdictComment`, `postSlotOnce`, `lock.ts`) with the two-guard implementation, or restore guard 3.\n3. Optional: drop dead `superseded` plumbing in `postSlotOnce` if stale tags are an accepted trade.\n\n## Skills\n\n| Skill | Found | Changed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| adversarial-review | Traced `postSlotOnce` / `postVerdictComment` through `ranOnce` → `claim` → `run` → `postComment`; push-between-read-and-write no longer blocked for tags | — |\n| owner-rules | Docs must match runtime guarantees; asymmetric stale protection tag vs verdict | — |\n| settled-check | No locks/roadmap in fixture tree for this path; race accepted in `lock.ts` prose but tag stale guard was explicit pre-diff | — |",
