      "finalText": "**MODIFY** posted on PR 9304 at `8696641c233071dab4f6d2976610b82e4d4e76a3`.\n\nThe change does what the title says: it removes two GitHub reads before writes. That trades latency and API cost against races you called out (multi-process, no KV CAS, push while a post is in flight).\n\n**Main issues**\n\n1. **Stale slot tags (P2, inferred)** — `isStale` was removed from `postSlotOnce`, so `@cursor` / `@replit` tags can still be created after a synchronize or draft flip, with a marker for the old head. `postVerdictComment` still runs `isStale` inside `run`, so tags and verdicts are no longer aligned. The `superseded` path in `tagOutcome` is unreachable while comments still say superseded heads must not be tagged.\n\n2. **Docs vs code (P2, measured)** — The module header, `postVerdictComment` “three guards” block, `postSlotOnce` superseded wording, and `lock.ts` still describe an immediate pre-write comment re-read that guard 3 used to provide; that re-read is gone from this diff, and `lock.ts` was not updated.\n\n3. **Duplicate MC verdicts (P3, inferred)** — Dropping guard 3 widens the cross-process double-post window; that may be an acceptable trade if comments state it plainly.\n\n**To reach PASS:** restore a single stale-head check before tag writes, or document and wire `tagOutcome` for accepting stale tag posts; and bring all comments (including `lock.ts`) in line with the two-guard behavior—or put guard 3 back.\n\nGate fixture still reports `next: review` / `mc: missing` (likely fixture lag after `post_verdict`); the formal MODIFY comment is on the PR.",
